## Stakeholder Matrix & Conflict Resolution Strategy

| Stakeholder Role | Power | Interest | Key Requirements | Engagement / Design Strategy |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Community Requesters** | Low | High | Simple intake form, unique tracking IDs, status notifications, mobile access. | **Keep Informed:** Clean UI, automated notifications, transparent status history. |
| **Field Maintenance Staff** | Medium | High | Clear task queues, location details, resolution notes, low admin overhead. | **Keep Satisfied:** Fast status updates, mobile-friendly task filtering. |
| **Administrative / Helpdesk** | High | Medium | Triage requests, reassign tasks, edit category configs, user account management. | **Manage Closely:** Granular RBAC permissions, batch assignment tools. |
| **Executive Management** | High | High | Summary dashboards, SLA compliance metrics, historical reports, bottleneck tracking. | **Key Player:** Automated reporting views, overdue task alerts. |
| **System Developers / IT Ops** | Medium | Medium | Maintainable codebase, R0 spend, sub-2s query speeds, secure RBAC. | **Monitor/Support:** Strict architectural patterns, CI pipelines, indexed queries. |

### Stakeholder Conflict Resolution

* **Requesters vs. Field Staff:** Requesters demand immediate resolution for all logged issues, whereas Field Staff face fixed workforce limits. CivicConnect introduces objective, automated SLA categorization upon intake. Issues receive predefined priority levels and target resolution windows. Requesters see clear status states (Open, In Progress, Overdue, Resolved) rather than vague promises.
* **Management vs. Field Staff:** Management requires strict oversight and detailed reporting, while Field Staff resist time-consuming administrative data entry. Workflow updates are streamlined into single-action status transitions with optional quick-notes. Mandatory metadata (timestamps, user IDs, status deltas) is captured automatically by the backend audit engine.

